speechtext:
Mr. JEAN-FRANCOIS POULIOT (Temis-couata) moved:
Whereas the city of Riviere-du-Loup is the most important centre in the eastern part of the province of Quebec;
And whereas, the harbour of said city is free from ice in winter, and navigation is possible at all times of the year;
And whereas, the said city is a divisional point of the national railways and the terminus of the Temiscouata railway, which links the said city to the province of New Brunswick, and to that part of the national railways known in the past as the transcontinental;
And whereas, the national railways are still in possession of the branch line of Riviere-du-Loup station to the Rivi6re-du-Loup wharf;
And whereas, the transport by water of coal from Nova Scotia to Riviere-du-Loup would be much more economical and would permit of its

Pacific Coast Shipping-Mr. Reid
distribution at a lower cost in eastern Quebec, in the northern part of New Brunswick and the state of Maine;
Therefore be it resolved, that, in the opinion ot this house, the government should appoint experts to study the matter from every angle and report to the government as soon °as possible.
He said: Mr. Speaker, in order to be very brief, because I have learned that action is preferable to speeches, I would refer the house to Hansard of April 27, 1931. Hon. members will find there a debate on a similar motion and also a very good reason why they should adopt this one. That motion was voted down by the then Prime Minister (Mr. Bennett) and fourteen or fifteen of his ministers who voted against it. Therefore, it must be a very good measure and I recommend it highly to the house. I shall not say any more, as I am sure that the Minister of Railways and Canals (Mr. Howe), who is also Minister of Marine, will accept this resolution.
